        },
        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "## 1 Introduction",
            "block_bbox": [
                140,
                725,
                368,
                753
            ],
            "block_id": 7,
            "block_order": 7,
            "group_id": 7,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    140.0,
                    725.0
                ],
                [
                    368.0,
                    725.0
                ],
                [
                    368.0,
                    753.0
                ],
                [
                    140.0,
                    753.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "In physical problems, as well as in problems of chemistry, mechanics, biology, and economics, it is necessary to estimate parameters of dynamical systems [1]. This is precisely the main question of parametric identification theory. Nevertheless, parameter estimation algorithms are often invented outside the cybernetics journals, for example, in physics papers [2, 3], whenever a corresponding need arises.",
            "block_bbox": [
                136,
                777,
                1084,
                911
            ],
            "block_id": 8,
            "block_order": 8,
            "group_id": 8,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    136.0,
                    777.0
                ],
                [
                    1084.0,
                    777.0
                ],
                [
                    1084.0,
                    911.0
                ],
                [
                    136.0,
                    911.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Besides inventing a method for parameter estimation, it is important to be certain that the obtained estimates are accurate and correct. The performance criterion for identification algorithms is usually the persistent excitation (PE) condition and its various modifications: weak PE, interval excitation, etc. [4–7].",
            "block_bbox": [
                136,
                914,
                1083,
                1019
            ],
            "block_id": 9,
            "block_order": 9,
            "group_id": 9,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    136.0,
                    914.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    914.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    1019.0
                ],
                [
                    136.0,
                    1019.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "However, a paradox arises here. First, the PE condition is not easy to prove even for known parameter values, because one needs to verify an inequality for the integral of a function along the trajectory. Second, verifying the PE condition is of interest before the parameters are identified; consequently, one has to work with a system where the parameters are unknown. Coping with these difficulties is hard, and often even in very strong papers the PE condition is introduced as an assumption [8–10] or the question of identifiability is not raised [2]. A priori ways to verify the PE condition are poorly developed [11], and publications on this topic are rare [11–13]. The aim of the present paper is to propose a method to solve these problems for a certain class of systems.",
            "block_bbox": [
                136,
                1022,
                1083,
                1236
            ],
            "block_id": 10,
            "block_order": 10,
            "group_id": 10,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    136.0,
                    1022.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    1022.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    1236.0
                ],
                [
                    136.0,
                    1236.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "When identifying parameters of complex autonomous nonlinear systems with higher-order interactions, the question becomes especially acute. In classical approaches, PE is often provided by an input signal (for instance, a sum of sinusoids is fed into the input); however, for a complex system",
            "block_bbox": [
                138,
                1240,
                1084,
                1319
            ],
            "block_id": 11,
            "block_order": 11,
            "group_id": 11,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
